1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,342
It's two in the morning, and checkout is failing.

2
00:00:03,342 --> 00:00:06,455
In ten minutes, three changes went live. Which one broke it?

3
00:00:06,455 --> 00:00:09,089
Roll back the wrong one, and it gets worse.

4
00:00:09,089 --> 00:00:11,132
The answer lives in the connections.

5
00:00:11,132 --> 00:00:16,185
Dashboards show symptoms, and today's AI agents answer with confidence, not proof.

6
00:00:16,185 --> 00:00:19,619
What if an agent could only blame what it can prove as a path?

7
00:00:19,619 --> 00:00:24,737
That's Faultline. No path, no claim. And the engine that makes it possible is FalkorDB.

8
00:00:24,737 --> 00:00:31,266
This cube is a real system's graph. Open it: services, deploys, people, and the proof path between them.

9
00:00:31,266 --> 00:00:33,854
Inside FalkorDB, alerts, deploys, lineage,

10
00:00:33,854 --> 00:00:39,395
docs and people stream into one graph per customer, each with its own read-only user.

11
00:00:39,395 --> 00:00:49,570
Every relationship is a sparse matrix, walked with linear algebra: 296 thousand nodes and 1.1 million edges fit in 490 megabytes.

12
00:00:49,570 --> 00:00:56,647
Checkout alerts. A breadth-first search spreads out inside the database: the blast radius, in a fraction of a millisecond.

13
00:00:56,647 --> 00:01:02,488
Three deploys landed. For the recommendation deploy, the shortest-path procedure returns zero rows.

14
00:01:02,488 --> 00:01:04,677
No path, so it cannot be blamed.

15
00:01:04,677 --> 00:01:10,836
But a path is not enough. Max flow shows the email deploy can carry only 2 percent of checkout's traffic.

16
00:01:10,836 --> 00:01:12,368
Rejected, with proof.

17
00:01:12,368 --> 00:01:19,082
The minimum cut is the smallest containment, tried first on a graph copy made in 383 milliseconds.

18
00:01:19,082 --> 00:01:23,979
Centrality ranks who should fix it, and a spanning forest draws the escalation chain.

19
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:30,029
The model can only point: every read is read-only, and one atomic write records a human's approval.

20
00:01:30,029 --> 00:01:36,543
All 10 graph algorithms change a decision. Without max flow, the right cause drops from 20 of 20 to 0.

21
00:01:36,543 --> 00:01:41,834
Switch FalkorDB off, and Faultline confirms nothing. FalkorDB is the brain.

22
00:01:41,834 --> 00:01:47,171
Now, live. The incident opens, and a crew of agents gets to work: recall, scope, suspects.

23
00:01:47,171 --> 00:01:49,247
Every step lands in the graph.

24
00:01:49,247 --> 00:01:54,171
The gate rejects the recommendation deploy: the exact Cypher, and its zero rows.

25
00:01:54,171 --> 00:01:58,110
The email deploy's path is too narrow: max flow rejects it.

26
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:03,411
The payment deploy passes. Here's the proof path, and every piece of evidence behind it.

27
00:02:04,488 --> 00:02:08,409
The smallest containment, tested on a copy, and who should fix it.

28
00:02:08,409 --> 00:02:11,938
A human approves the exact change, bound to its plan hash.

29
00:02:11,938 --> 00:02:16,379
Faultline opens a real pull request, merges it, and verifies it. Applied.

30
00:02:16,379 --> 00:02:19,838
Then it writes the decision back into FalkorDB.

31
00:02:38,324 --> 00:02:43,614
Faultline also runs the pager. It pages whoever the graph says is on call for the owning team.

32
00:02:43,614 --> 00:02:47,703
On their phone: the verdict, the proof path, and who fixes it.

33
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:52,044
One tap acknowledges it, and the escalation stops.

34
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:56,835
After a verified fix, the on-call can grant a standing approval: one use, seven days.

35
00:02:56,835 --> 00:03:03,374
When the same fault comes back, the gate still proves the cause, and a second real pull request merges and verifies.

36
00:03:03,374 --> 00:03:06,398
Handled while you slept. Zero people woken.

37
00:03:22,112 --> 00:03:25,367
It's the team's memory, too. Who decided the payment timeout?

38
00:03:25,367 --> 00:03:27,968
Every name in the answer comes from a query.

39
00:03:27,968 --> 00:03:33,970
And it learns from the people using it: the fixes your team prefers, the guidelines they set, who actually answers.

40
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:37,874
The next plan puts your team's choice first: moved up from number two.

41
00:03:37,874 --> 00:03:44,025
And FalkorDB shows its own work: the memory each graph takes, every query's timing, and the live tail.

42
00:03:44,025 --> 00:03:52,571
The numbers, honestly. On 140 worlds built to fool it: 0 confident wrong blames, the right cause in 51 of 100, and not sure on the rest.

43
00:03:52,571 --> 00:04:03,065
A strong language model blamed an innocent change 37 times. On a real system, the OpenTelemetry Demo: the right cause in 11 of 20 faults, 0 wrong blames in 40 windows.

44
00:04:03,065 --> 00:04:05,835
A telemetry threshold was wrong 8 times.

45
00:04:05,835 --> 00:04:11,027
Refusing is cheap: no path in 0.053 milliseconds, and zero tokens.

46
00:04:11,027 --> 00:04:15,843
And it scales: 250 customer graphs on one engine, 0 leaks.

47
00:04:15,843 --> 00:04:18,736
It's live now: 5 of 5 real runs, verified.

48
00:04:18,736 --> 00:04:23,833
Any agent can use the same tools over M.C.P. Faultline, built on FalkorDB.

49
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:25,672
No path, no claim.

